14. Execution policy

Before executing generated code:

Confirm that all referenced tools exist.
Check that their arguments match documented schemas.
Check that the workflow respects user intent.
Check for unintended side effects.
Check that the program does not access unauthorized resources.
Check that resource limits are appropriate.

If the platform does not provide a secure execution environment, fall back to native orchestration.

Never use unrestricted eval or Function execution as a substitute for a security sandbox.

15. Output format

Return a structured result internally:

typescript
interface WorkflowResult {
  success: boolean;
  summary: string;
  data?: unknown;
  errors?: Array<{
    step: string;
    message: string;
  }>;
  execution?: {
    completedSteps: number;
    failedSteps: number;
  };
}


The user-facing response should contain:

The completed outcome.
Important findings.
Relevant limitations.
Any action that requires further authorization.

Do not expose internal implementation details unless requested.

16. When to activate this skill

Activate when the user asks to:

Perform multi-step work across connected applications.
Retrieve and combine information from multiple APIs.
Process large API responses.
Automate repetitive workflows.
Coordinate several MCP servers.
Generate reports from connected data.
Execute complex tool-dependent operations.
Build an MCP orchestration runtime.

For a simple single-tool task, use the tool directly. Do not introduce unnecessary code generation.

17. Success criteria

A workflow is successful when:

The requested outcome has been achieved.
All tool calls were authorized.
Inputs and outputs were validated.
Dependencies were respected.
Failures were handled transparently.
Unnecessary intermediate context was avoided.
No unauthorized network or resource access occurred.
The final result is accurate and verifiable.

Core principle: Give the agent a programmable interface to its tools, let code handle orchestration, and let the model focus on reasoning and the final result.
